{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the third month, etc.</b> Now, given the surpassing love of the Blessed One for Israel and the greatness of His longing to give them this Torah as their betrothed, it is difficult: why did HaShem delay giving the Torah until the third month? For one of the signs of love is that longing is not delayed from reaching its desired object. And if the reason is the exigencies of travel — behold, we find that even for Eliezer, the servant of Abraham, the earth was miraculously contracted for him (Sanhedrin 95a) when he went to take a wife for Isaac, and all the more so by a fortiori reasoning for the marriage of the Prince of the universe, the earth would contract — and even the heavens if need be." "Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, came and provided a pretext for the matter: it was not from any diminution of longing that caused the delay, but rather for the purpose of preparing the groom, for Israel was not fit, inasmuch as they were in an impure land and were in a state of menstrual impurity among themselves. They needed to count the count of their purification — seven sabbaths — in the manner of the seven clean days that HaShem commanded for the zavah (Zohar III:97a). This is what the verse means by <b>the going out of the children of Israel</b> — meaning, the cause of their going out from the land of Egypt is what occasioned the delay of the matter until the third month. And the proof is that when the days of their preparation drew near, on that very day they set out and on that very day they arrived, which is what is meant by <b>on this day</b> — meaning that they traveled on that day and arrived on that day, and so it is brought in the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Shabbat 87a) that on the day they traveled they arrived. And I looked into the matter and saw the surpassing longing of the Blessed One for Israel, revealed through His actions, by raising the question of why HaShem had to trouble them on the journey until the third month. And even for the reason we have written, it remains difficult: He should have contracted the earth for them and arrived at the wilderness of Sinai on that very day, and there they would tarry to count seven weeks, and the time of fitness would be as mentioned." "<b>Truly,</b> this indicates the surpassing longing to give the Torah to Israel, for because of the greatness of His longing, blessed be He, toward them, He did not wish to bring them and remain a long time without the wedding in the wedding house. Therefore He minimized the days by shortening the distance. And you may take the measure of these matters from the analogy of a man and his longing for his beloved: he does not suffer distress at being held back from coming to her, as long as the time of love has not yet arrived and the bride has not yet gone up to her canopy — unlike at the time when the bride is in her canopy, when he cannot bear the restraint of delaying his coming to her."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:51.680376899Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}